# WHAT SHIPS TO THE DEPLOY. Railway reads this INSTEAD of .gitignore when it exists, which is
# the entire reason it exists here: `seed_runs/` is deliberately gitignored — it holds a copy
# of the operator's corpus and must never be committed to a public repository — and Railway
# was therefore skipping it too. The seeding path in `ui/boot.py` has consequently never
# shipped anything, so a volume that took its snapshot on first boot kept it forever, and the
# deploy served material 1.2 days and 50,000 arrows behind local under a commit stamp that
# matched HEAD exactly.
#
# So: everything .gitignore excludes for repository hygiene stays excluded here, and the one
# category that is excluded for PUBLICATION rather than for hygiene — the corpus staging copy —
# ships. The distinction is the point: not-in-git and not-on-the-deploy are different
# decisions, and conflating them silently disabled a mechanism nobody could see was off.

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.venv/
venv/
node_modules/
